---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Meyppi is an independent media delivery verification company based in India. From Tamil mei (மெய்), truth. What we verify, how we work, and what we refuse to claim."
>
  <section class="about-head">
    <div class="container about-head__grid">
      <div>
        <span class="kicker">About</span>
        <h1>Verification that starts from the media itself.</h1>
        <p>
          Meyppi is an independent media delivery verification company. We
          take a campaign you have already paid for, go back to the
          published media, and establish what actually ran.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-head__mark" aria-hidden="true">
        <Logo variant="mark" width={280} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">

      <h2>Why this company exists</h2>
      <p>
        Audio advertising in India grew up fast. Budgets moved into podcasts
        and digital audio, insertion orders got longer, and the reporting
        stayed exactly where it was in 2015: a spreadsheet from the
        publisher, a summary from the agency, and a number everyone agrees
        to believe.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of the time that number is fine. But nobody involved is
        incentivised to check it, and the few people who might want to,
        a brand manager, an agency's client-lead, have no way to do it.
        The published episodes are out there in public RSS feeds. The
        creatives are sitting in the brand's own asset library. Everything
        needed to verify delivery already exists. It is just never brought
        together.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole job. Meyppi brings it together: your contracted
        creatives on one side, the published media on the other, and a
        fingerprinting pipeline in between that says, for every expected
        placement, whether it ran, which version ran, at what point in the
        episode, and for how long.
      </p>

      <div class="pull">
        <p>மெய்</p>
        <cite>
          mei (Tamil): truth; that which is real. The word the company is
          named after, and the standard the reports are held to.
        </cite>
      </div>

      <h2>The name</h2>
      <p>
        Meyppi comes from the Tamil word <em>mei</em>, மெய். It means truth,
        or more precisely, that which is real. Not truth as an opinion or a
        promise. Truth as verifiable fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        The name is a constraint as much as a brand. If a report from this
        company says a placement ran for 24 of 30 contracted seconds, that
        statement has to survive being checked. It is backed by the file
        hashes, match offsets and raw evidence that ship with every audit,
        or it does not get made. Where the evidence is thin, the report
        says so and the placement is marked for manual review instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        We picked a word that would embarrass us if we cut corners.
      </p>

      <h2>What actually happens in an audit</h2>
      <p>
        The pipeline is boring on purpose. You send the master creative
        files, every version that was contracted, along with the placement
        plan: which publishers, which episodes, which dates. We collect the
        published media from RSS feeds and other permitted sources, or from
        recordings you already hold, and we record where each file came
        from.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each creative and each episode is then fingerprinted. A
        fingerprint, in this context, is a compact mathematical description
        of what a piece of audio sounds like, robust to compression and
        background noise, computed the same way every time. Matching the
        creative's fingerprint against the episode's is what finds the ad:
        the same technique that lets a phone identify a song from a second
        of audio in a noisy room. Ours is tuned for one question only. Is
        this contracted 30-second cut present in this episode, completely,
        partially, or not at all?
      </p>
      <p>
        The classification layer then does the forensic part. It measures
        how much of the creative's runtime the match actually covers,
        whether the beginning or end is missing, and which version of the
        creative the evidence points to when several versions exist. Every
        measurement becomes a field in the report. Nothing is reduced to a
        bare score.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will not claim</h2>
      <p>
        Some things an audit from us will never tell you, because no honest
        process could.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is not legal proof. It is evidence, gathered and documented in a
        repeatable way, suitable for reconciling with a publisher or
        supporting a client conversation. Courts have their own standards
        and we do not pretend to meet them.
      </p>
      <p>
        It cannot verify what it cannot observe. If an episode was never
        published anywhere we are permitted to access, the honest answer is
        "Insufficient Signal", and that is the answer you will get. We do
        not compensate for missing media with cleverer algorithms, and we
        do not scrape sources we are not authorised to use.
      </p>
      <p>
        And in V1 it covers pre-produced audio creatives in podcast and
        digital audio. Dynamic ad insertion, host-read spots and video are
        real problems that deserve real solutions later. They are not
        quietly lumped into a vague "verified" tick.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>
        Meyppi is not part of any agency, publisher, network or adtech
        vendor, and does not buy or sell media. The only party we work for
        is the one who commissioned the audit. That is not a slogan; it is
        the entire commercial model. A verification company with a media
    side business is a verification company with a conflict of interest.
      </p>

      <p class="note">
        Meyppi is based in India and works with agencies and brands on
        podcast and digital audio campaigns. If you want to know whether
        your last campaign ran as contracted,
        <a href="/contact">that conversation starts here</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-head {
    background: var(--indigo-deep);
    background-image: var(--grid-dots);
    padding: clamp(56px, 7vw, 88px) 0;
    color: var(--white);
  }
  .about-head .kicker {
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .about-head h1 {
    color: var(--white);
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .about-head p {
    color: #c9d3e4;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 54ch;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .about-head__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 0.8fr;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .about-head__mark {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .prose em {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--teal-dark);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .pull p {
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 5vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .about-head__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
